course: history
lesson: 82
title: How writing began: from tally marks and pictures to the alphabet
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In Kipling's «Just So Stories» a Stone Age girl called Taffy draws a picture-letter that everybody reads wrongly, and then she and her father make up an alphabet in a single afternoon. It is a lovely story, but real writing took thousands of years and many peoples to grow.
explain: People painted animals in caves tens of thousands of years ago, but a picture is not yet writing: it does not tell you the exact words, so two people can read it two different ways, just as Taffy's mother did.
explain: True writing began about 5,200 years ago in Mesopotamia and Egypt, when temple workers and traders needed to remember how much grain, beer and how many sheep they had. Later it was invented again, on its own, in China and in Central America.
explain: The biggest trick came next: using a picture for its SOUND, not its meaning. From that idea came the alphabet, a small set of letters that can write any word at all, and the letters you are reading now are its great-grandchildren.
fact: People painted animals on cave walls more than 40,000 years ago, but pictures alone are not writing, because they do not show the exact words.
fact: For thousands of years before writing, people in the Middle East counted goods with small clay tokens and tally marks; the first writing grew out of these counting records.
fact: In Mesopotamia (now mostly Iraq) the Sumerians began writing about 5,200 years ago, pressing a reed into wet clay; Egypt had hieroglyphs at about the same time.
fact: Writing was invented again, separately, in China about 3,200 years ago (on «oracle bones») and in Mexico and Central America more than 2,000 years ago, where the Maya script is the best known.
fact: The rebus trick uses a picture for its sound: a bee and a leaf can spell «belief». The first writers in Egypt and Mesopotamia used it to write names and words that cannot be drawn.
fact: About 3,800 years ago, workers in Egypt and Sinai who spoke a language related to Hebrew and Arabic took Egyptian picture-signs and used each one only for the first sound of its name: this was the first alphabet.
fact: That is why our A began as an ox's head («alp» meant ox), B as a house («bet»), M as wavy water («mem») and O as an eye.
fact: The Phoenicians were sea-traders from cities such as Tyre, Sidon and Byblos in today's Lebanon, famous for a costly purple dye made from sea snails; about 3,000 years ago they spread a 22-letter alphabet around the Mediterranean.
fact: The Phoenician alphabet had letters only for consonants, not for vowels; the Hebrew and Arabic scripts grew from the same family and are still often written without vowels.
fact: The Greeks borrowed the alphabet about 2,800 years ago and added vowels; the word «alphabet» comes from their first two letters, alpha and beta.
fact: The Romans took the alphabet from the Greeks and the Etruscans; English uses their Latin alphabet, and the Cyrillic alphabet of Ukrainian grew from the Greek one about 1,100 years ago.
fact: Byblos sold so much papyrus that the Greeks called papyrus and books after the city, and from their word for «book» comes the word «Bible».
fact: Chinese writing never became an alphabet: it uses thousands of characters, and a reader needs about three thousand of them for a newspaper, while English needs 26 letters.
fact: Stone Age people in Britain did not write; writing came to Britain with the Romans about 2,000 years ago, so Taffy and Tegumai in Surrey could not really have done it.
exercise: Why is a cave painting not yet writing? | It does not show the exact words | It was painted by children | It has no colours at all | It shows only animals that died out
exercise: What did the first writing in Mesopotamia mostly record? | Grain, sheep and beer for temples and traders | Love poems and long adventure stories about gods | The names of all the stars in the night sky | Laws about how to build a pyramid
exercise: What came before writing for counting goods in the Middle East? | Small clay tokens and tally marks | Silver coins stamped with the faces of kings | Paper notebooks made from rice | Knotted strings, as in Peru
exercise: About how long ago did writing begin in Mesopotamia and Egypt? | About 5,200 years ago | About 500 years ago | About 50,000 years ago | About 2,000 years ago
exercise: Where was writing invented again, all on its own? | In China and in Central America | In Britain and in Ireland | In Australia and in New Zealand | In Iceland and in Greenland
exercise: What is the rebus trick? | Using a picture for its sound, not its meaning | Writing every word backwards so that only friends can read it | Drawing a picture very small so it fits on clay | Using colours to show which words are important
exercise: Who made the first alphabet? | Workers in Egypt and Sinai, about 3,800 years ago | Roman soldiers who came to Britain, about 2,000 years ago | Greek teachers in Athens, about 2,800 years ago | A Stone Age family living in Surrey, in England
exercise: What picture did our letter A begin as? | An ox's head | An arrow on a bow | A mountain | A tent
exercise: What did our letter M begin as? | Wavy water | Two mountains | A bird | A man's face
exercise: Who were the Phoenicians? | Sea-traders from cities in today's Lebanon | Farmers from the green Sahara, long ago | Kings of Egypt who built the great pyramids | Horse riders from the steppe
exercise: What were the Phoenicians famous for selling? | A costly purple dye made from sea snails | Tea and fine silk from faraway China | Gold and silver dug from the mines of Britain | Paper money printed on rice paper
exercise: What did the Phoenician alphabet not have? | Letters for vowels | Letters for consonants | More than ten letters | A letter for B
exercise: What did the Greeks add to the alphabet? | Vowels | Numbers | Spaces | Capital letters
exercise: Where does the word «alphabet» come from? | Alpha and beta, the first Greek letters | The name of the Egyptian man who made it | A Latin word for «writing on wet clay» | A Phoenician word for «sea»
exercise: Which alphabet does English use? | The Latin alphabet of the Romans | The Greek alphabet of Athens | The Phoenician alphabet of Tyre | The Cyrillic alphabet
exercise: When did writing come to Britain? | With the Romans, about 2,000 years ago | In the Stone Age, with Taffy | With the Vikings, about 1,000 years ago | Only when printing came, about 550 years ago
test: Taffy sends a picture-letter and her mother thinks Tegumai has been hurt. What does this show about pictures? | They can be read in different ways, unlike words | Pictures always mean exactly one thing to everybody who sees them | Mothers cannot read pictures at all | Pictures are faster to read than any letters
test: «lol writing was invented by one clever guy one day, like in the book» What is closer to the truth? | It grew slowly over thousands of years, in several places | One Egyptian king invented every letter in a single year | A girl and her dad really did make it up in one day | It was invented by the Romans, who taught it to everyone
test: You draw an eye, a can and a sea to write «I can see». Which trick is that? | The rebus trick of pictures for sounds | The alphabet trick of one letter per sound | The tally trick of counting with marks | The cave trick of drawing what you saw
test: Turn a capital A upside down. Which old picture might you see? | An ox's head with two horns | A house with a door | A snake curled round an egg | A fish with its mouth open
test: ★★ A Phoenician ship sails from Tyre to Carthage and Greece with dye and letters. Why did the alphabet spread so fast? | Traders sailed everywhere and needed a quick, easy way to write | Every king ordered his people to learn it in one day | It was carved on the ships so everybody saw it | The Phoenicians fought wars to make everyone use it
test: «Chinese is just an alphabet with loads of letters innit» What is right? | No, it uses thousands of characters, not 26 letters | Yes, it has exactly 26 letters like English | Yes, it is the Greek alphabet drawn in a different way | No, Chinese is not written down at all
test: «my teacher says the Greeks invented the alphabet» What is the fairer answer? | They borrowed it from the Phoenicians and added vowels | Yes, the Greeks made every single letter from nothing | No, the Romans gave it to the Greeks | No, it came from China along the Silk Road
test: Why could Kipling's Tegumai not really have written letters in Stone Age Surrey? | Writing reached Britain only with the Romans, thousands of years later | There was no birch-bark anywhere in Stone Age Britain then | Stone Age people were not clever enough to learn | It always rained too hard to write outside
test: Which pair of scripts both grew from the old alphabet of the Phoenicians? | Latin and Arabic | Chinese and Maya | Egyptian hieroglyphs and cuneiform | Chinese and Japanese
test: ★★ A Phoenician word «brd» could be read «bread», «bird», «bard» or «board». Which problem do the missing letters cause? | Without vowels a word can be read in several ways | Without vowels you cannot write any word at all | Without vowels words become far too long to write | Without vowels nobody could say the word out loud
test: ★★★ The Phoenicians had 22 letters. The Greeks added 5 new letters at the end and later dropped 3 old ones they did not need. How many letters has the Greek alphabet? | 24 | 27 | 22 | 30
test: «writing came from cave paintings, cave people wrote stories on the walls» What would you say? | Cave paintings are much older but are pictures, not writing; writing began with counting records | Yes, cave people wrote whole stories in letters on the walls, which we can still read today | No, cave paintings were made after writing was invented | Yes, every cave painting can be read word for word today
exam: Identify the principal purpose for which writing was first employed in Mesopotamia. | The recording of quantities of grain, livestock and other goods held by temples and traders | The composition of poetry for the entertainment of kings | The drawing of maps for travellers by sea | The teaching of children to read in schools
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the earliest alphabet? | Picture-signs were employed solely for the first sound of their names | Each sign denoted an entire word or idea, as in the writing of China | Each sign denoted a vowel and never a consonant | It comprised several thousand signs
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Phoenicians. | They were seafaring merchants of the eastern Mediterranean who disseminated a consonantal alphabet | They were the inventors of the pyramids, who wrote solely by means of hieroglyphs | They were a people of China who invented paper | They were Roman soldiers who brought writing to Britain
exam: Ascertain the people to whom the alphabet owes the addition of letters for vowels. | The Greeks | The Phoenicians | The Sumerians | The Egyptians
